  "query_preview": "..\nTonight through Monday (Labor Day): A large closed low will\nbe the main player in the weather forecast through the next\nseveral days, all the way through Labor Day. A large closed low\njust off Vancouver Island today will drop southeast to just off\nthe Oregon coast on Thursday. A stronger mid level wave\nassociated with this low will rotate northeast across the region\nthis evening into Thursday morning bringing widespread \nbeneficial rain showers. The highest rain totals will occur over\nNE Washington into the ID Panhandle as well as the Cascade \ncrest with added low level upslope flow. Rain totals for most \nareas range from a tenth to quarter inch, with less than a tenth\nthe Wenatchee area into the western basin. Gusty winds ahead of\nthis band of showers will lead to the potential for blowing dust\nwith southerly winds gusting to 25-35 MPH. Following this band \nof showers a mid level dry slot swings through the region on \nThursday resulting in showers mainly being confined to the \nCascades, as well as far SE Washington into the Central \nPanhandle Mountains. Friday through Labor Day regional coverage \nof showers will be on the increase as the low moves over the \nregion. Yet the models show most of the shower activity to be \nisolated to scattered in nature. There will also be a risk for \nisolated thunderstorms each afternoon and evening mainly in the \nmountains with uncapped CAPE values ranging from 200-600 J/KG. \nStorm motion will be slow over Central Washington so will have \nto monitor the burn scars in the Okanogan Highlands and East \nSlopes of the Cascades for isolated downpours from storms. With \nthe cloud cover and shower activity temperatures will remain \nbelow normal with highs in the mid 60s to mid 70s. \n\nTuesday and Wednesday: A warming and drying trend is expected as\nthe low pulls away from the region. This will allow high\ntemperatures for next Wednesday to warm to near normal values\nwith mid 70s to low 80s. JW",
